<onlyinclude>'''High-bandwidth memory''' (HBM) is a kind of computer memory that stacks several memory chips and connects them to a processor through a very wide connection. This helps move large amounts of data quickly, especially in graphics, scientific computing, and AI accelerators.</onlyinclude>

== How it works ==
Imagine several floors of memory connected vertically, placed close to a processor. Many data connections can operate in parallel, reducing the need to send all the data through a narrow interface.

HBM is still DRAM: it stores information temporarily and needs refreshing. Its main advantage is ''bandwidth'' (how much data can be transferred per second), not necessarily faster response to each individual request.

== Tradeoffs ==
HBM requires sophisticated packaging and cooling, can cost more than conventional memory arrangements, and has finite capacity. Actual speed depends on the program and memory controller.

